{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"INDEPENDENT REVIEW OF CHANGE 1209 BY A SECOND SESSION: NO ASSERTION WAS RELAXED. Checked\nspecifically because the operator's standing instruction on this ticket was 'do not resolve it by\nrelaxing the assertions', and because the fix necessarily touched two orchestrate cells that were\nfailing. Reviewed patchset 2 (13316a0ab1) against origin/main df41edcf25.\n\nWHOLE-CHANGE ASSERTION LEDGER: 19 assert lines ADDED, 1 REMOVED. Net +18.\n\nTHE ONE REMOVAL, examined closely because this is exactly where a relaxation would hide.\nRemoved: 'assert result2[\"mutation_count\"] == 1' from\norchestrate/test_reconcile_once.py::test_real_field_change_converges_after_one_pass.\nThat assertion was PINNING THE DEFECT, not the behaviour its own docstring claimed. The fixture\nchanges the summary on the JIRA side only; the harness has local_tickets=[], so the single mutation\nit counted was an outbound update carrying the STALE PREVIOUS value — a REVERT of the fixture's own\nedit. Keeping it would have required the phantom to keep being emitted.\n\nWHAT REPLACED IT — two assertions, and together they are strictly stronger than the one removed:\n  * BOTH HALVES OF THE ORIGINAL INTENT ARE PRESERVED. The docstring's 'regression guard against\n    over-filtering' now lives at pass 1: 'assert result1[\"mutation_count\"] >= 1' — a key the\n    reconciler has never seen is genuine work and must still be planned, so a suppression broad\n    enough to swallow real work FAILS THERE. That is the guard the removed line was nominally\n    providing, relocated to a pass where it is true.\n  * A NEW STRICT ASSERTION AT PASS 2: 'assert result2[\"mutation_count\"] == 0', with a failure\n    message naming the mechanism ('the only thing the snapshot differ can emit here is an outbound\n    update carrying the STALE previous value — a revert of the edit, not a sync of it'). == 0 is\n    tighter than == 1, not looser.\n  * The docstring records WHAT IT USED TO ASSERT, the instrumented evidence that this was wrong\n    (the captured payload and provenance), and WHY zero planned work is correct here rather than\n    lost work — a Jira-side field edit is mirrored inbound by the BINDING-AWARE differ (ADR 0026's\n    arbitrated inbound-mirrored scalar set; ticket b9b8 and\n    diffing/test_inbound_field_sync_directionality.py), which needs a binding this snapshot-only\n    harness never creates.\n\nTHE SECOND TOUCHED CELL WAS FIXED BY MAKING IT NON-VACUOUS, AND IT GAINED AN ASSERTION.\norchestrate/test_reconcile_health_wiring.py::test_health_record_pass_called_after_apply compared an\nobserved count against len(mutations) where _make_stub_differ returned THE TEST'S OWN LIST OBJECT,\nwhich the diff phase appends to IN PLACE — so expectation and observation were the same object and\nthe cell passed for ANY number of appended mutations. The stub now returns a fresh copy (so the\nexpectation is a fixed value production cannot rewrite) and the snapshot is emptied for isolation,\nmatching what the sibling zero-mutation cell already relied on. A NEW assertion was added on top:\nhealth.record_pass()'s count and the returned result['mutation_count'] come from different plumbing\nand must agree.\n\nCONCLUSION: this satisfies the constraint. One assertion that encoded the bug was inverted with its\nintent preserved and its reasoning recorded in the docstring — the remedy AGENTS.md/handoff #6\nprescribes for a test that pins a defect — and eighteen net new assertions were added around it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785690712940075001,"uuid":"7a5594d1-25df-442d-9ca4-d59ee215c9bc"}